exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
V=[
X(1,'SEVEN QUESTIONS · I',
 S('Arjuna asking a list of questions','A','book','WHAT IS BRAHMAN?'),
 'Arjuna said: What is that Brahman? What is the inner Self (adhyātma)? What is action, best of persons? What is said to be the ground of beings (adhibhūta), and what the ground of the gods (adhidaiva)?',
 [('kim tat brahma','<b>what is that Brahman?</b>'),('kim adhyātmam','<b>what is the inner Self?</b>'),('kim karma puruṣottama','<b>what is action, best of persons?</b>'),('adhibhūtam ca kim proktam','<b>what is called adhibhūta?</b>'),('adhidaivam kim ucyate','<b>what is called adhidaiva?</b>')],
 'Chapter 7 ended with technical terms (7.29–30). Arjuna asks what they mean.',
 'Arjuna asks seven questions in two verses: six about terms and one about death. Commentators praise his alertness: a good student asks about every unclear word.',
 'Never pass over a word you don’t understand. Ask.',
 'Arjuna asks: <b>what do these words mean?</b>'),
X(2,'SEVEN QUESTIONS · II',
 S('Arjuna asking about the moment of death','A','death','AT DEATH?'),
 '“Who is the Lord of sacrifice (adhiyajña), and how is he here in this body, Madhusūdana? And how are you to be known at the time of death by the self-controlled?”',
 [('adhiyajñaḥ katham kaḥ atra','<b>who and how is adhiyajña here</b>'),('dehe asmin madhusūdana','<b>in this body</b>'),('prayāṇa-kāle ca katham','<b>and at the time of departure, how</b>'),('jñeyaḥ asi niyata-ātmabhiḥ','<b>are you known by the self-controlled?</b>')],
 'The last question is the most personal: how to know God at the moment of death.',
 'Commentators note the progression from abstract terms to the most practical question of all: how to die well.',
 'How we want to die says a lot about how we should live.',
 'How to know you <b>at the end?</b>'),
X(3,'THE DEFINITIONS · I',
 S('Krishna explaining: Brahman, svabhāva, karma','K','om','DEFINITIONS'),
 'The Lord said: Brahman is the Imperishable, the Supreme. Its own nature (svabhāva) is called adhyātma. The creative offering that brings beings into existence is called karma.',
 [('akṣaram brahma paramam','<b>Brahman is the supreme Imperishable</b>'),('svabhāvaḥ adhyātmam ucyate','<b>its own nature is called adhyātma</b>'),('bhūta-bhāva-udbhava-karaḥ','<b>that which brings beings into being</b>'),('visargaḥ karma-saṃjñitaḥ','<b>the offering is called karma</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa defines the first three terms.',
 'Commentators differ on adhyātma: Śaṅkara takes it as Brahman present in each body as the inner Self; Rāmānuja as the nature of the individual soul. Karma here means the cosmic creative act.',
 'Big words become simple when defined. Keep asking until they are.',
 'Brahman, Self, <b>karma defined</b>.'),
X(4,'THE DEFINITIONS · II',
 S('Krishna pointing to matter, the cosmic person and himself in the body','K','cosmic','ADHI-…'),
 '“Adhibhūta is perishable existence; adhidaiva is the cosmic Person (puruṣa); and I myself, here in the body, am adhiyajña, best of embodied beings.”',
 [('adhibhūtam kṣaraḥ bhāvaḥ','<b>adhibhūta is perishable existence</b>'),('puruṣaḥ ca adhidaivatam','<b>adhidaiva is the Puruṣa</b>'),('adhiyajñaḥ aham eva atra','<b>adhiyajña is I myself here</b>'),('dehe deha-bhṛtām vara','<b>in the body, best of the embodied</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa defines the rest: matter, the cosmic Person, and himself as Lord of sacrifice within the body.',
 'The Lord is present in each body as the receiver of all offerings. Commentators see this as God dwelling within, witnessing every act.',
 'God is not far away; he dwells in this very body.',
 'The Lord <b>dwells in the body</b>.'),
X(5,'REMEMBER ME AT THE END',
 S('A dying person peacefully remembering Krishna','KA','om','MĀM EVA SMARAN'),
 '“Whoever, at the time of death, leaves the body remembering me alone, reaches my being; there is no doubt of this.”',
 [('anta-kāle ca mām eva','<b>and at the end, me alone</b>'),('smaran muktvā kalevaram','<b>remembering, leaving the body</b>'),('yaḥ prayāti saḥ mat-bhāvam','<b>who departs, reaches my being</b>'),('yāti na asti atra saṃśayaḥ','<b>there is no doubt here</b>')],
 'The answer to the last question: remember me at the end.',
 'This verse is why many Hindus chant God’s name at a deathbed. Commentators add that remembrance at the end is the fruit of a whole life of remembrance.',
 'Practise remembering the Divine every day; it will be there at the end.',
 'Remember me <b>at the end</b>.'),
X(6,'WHAT YOU THINK OF, YOU BECOME',
 S('A person’s last thought shaping the next life','P','mind','TAD-BHĀVA'),
 '“Whatever state one remembers when leaving the body at the end, son of Kuntī, to that state one goes, always absorbed in it.”',
 [('yam yam vā api smaran bhāvam','<b>whatever state remembering</b>'),('tyajati ante kalevaram','<b>one leaves the body at the end</b>'),('tam tam eva eti kaunteya','<b>to that one goes</b>'),('sadā tat-bhāva-bhāvitaḥ','<b>always absorbed in that state</b>')],
 'The last thought shapes the next destination.',
 'Commentators tell the story of King Bharata, who in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa thought of a deer at death and was reborn as one. The point: the habits of a lifetime decide the last thought.',
 'What you dwell on daily is what you will dwell on at the end. Choose well.',
 'What you think of, <b>you become</b>.'),
X(7,'REMEMBER ME AND FIGHT',
 S('Arjuna fighting with Krishna in his heart','A','heart','SMARA · YUDHYA'),
 '“Therefore, at all times remember me, and fight. With mind and intellect offered to me, you will surely come to me.”',
 [('tasmāt sarveṣu kāleṣu','<b>therefore at all times</b>'),('mām anusmara yudhya ca','<b>remember me and fight</b>'),('mayi arpita-manaḥ-buddhiḥ','<b>mind and intellect offered to me</b>'),('mām eva eṣyasi asaṃśayam','<b>you will come to me, without doubt</b>')],
 'A balanced instruction: remember God and do your duty.',
 '“Remember me and fight”: not one or the other but both together. Commentators say this sums up the Gītā: devotion in action.',
 'Work with God in your heart. That is the whole method in four words.',
 '<b>Remember me, and fight</b>.'),
X(8,'PRACTICE MAKES IT STEADY',
 S('A meditator with a mind not wandering, eyes on a light','Y','light','ABHYĀSA'),
 '“With a mind trained by the practice of yoga, not straying elsewhere, meditating on the supreme divine Person, Pārtha, one goes to him.”',
 [('abhyāsa-yoga-yuktena','<b>trained by practice of yoga</b>'),('cetasā na anya-gāminā','<b>with a mind not going elsewhere</b>'),('paramam puruṣam divyam','<b>the supreme divine Person</b>'),('yāti pārtha anucintayan','<b>one goes, meditating</b>')],
 'Practice makes remembrance steady.',
 'Commentators link this to 6.35: practice tames the mind. Here it is directed at the Supreme Person.',
 'A little daily practice makes remembering natural.',
 'Practice <b>steadies remembrance</b>.'),
X(9,'THE ANCIENT SEER',
 S('A golden Person beyond darkness, like the sun','','sun','ĀDITYA-VARṆA'),
 '“One who meditates on the Seer, the Ancient, the Ruler, subtler than the subtle, the upholder of all, of form unthinkable, sun-coloured, beyond darkness,”',
 [('kavim purāṇam anuśāsitāram','<b>the Seer, Ancient, Ruler</b>'),('aṇoḥ aṇīyāṃsam','<b>subtler than the subtle</b>'),('sarvasya dhātāram','<b>upholder of all</b>'),('acintya-rūpam','<b>of unthinkable form</b>'),('āditya-varṇam tamasaḥ parastāt','<b>sun-coloured, beyond darkness</b>')],
 'A description of the Supreme to meditate on.',
 'These phrases echo the Upaniṣads (Śvetāśvatara 3.8: “sun-coloured, beyond darkness”). Commentators say each word is a meditation in itself. The sentence continues into 8.10.',
 'Choose one phrase, like “beyond darkness”, and hold it in mind for a few breaths.',
 'Sun-coloured, <b>beyond darkness</b>.'),
X(10,'BREATH BETWEEN THE BROWS',
 S('A yogi at the moment of death with breath gathered between the eyebrows','Y','light','BHRUVOḤ MADHYE'),
 '“…at the time of death, with a steady mind, joined in devotion and the strength of yoga, placing the life-breath between the eyebrows, he reaches that supreme divine Person.”',
 [('prayāṇa-kāle manasā acalena','<b>at death, with an unmoving mind</b>'),('bhaktyā yuktaḥ yoga-balena ca','<b>joined with devotion and the strength of yoga</b>'),('bhruvoḥ madhye prāṇam āveśya','<b>placing breath between the brows</b>'),('tam param puruṣam upaiti divyam','<b>reaches that supreme divine Person</b>')],
 'The yogi’s way of dying: steady mind, devotion, breath gathered upward.',
 'This is a traditional yogic practice for the moment of death. Commentators note that devotion is mentioned alongside yoga: technique alone is not enough.',
 'Even if we never practise such techniques, a steady, devoted mind is the essence.',
 'A steady mind <b>at the last breath</b>.'),
X(11,'THE STATE THEY SEEK',
 S('Sages walking toward an imperishable light','Y','om','AKṢARA'),
 '“I will tell you briefly of that state which knowers of the Veda call the Imperishable, which ascetics free from passion enter, and desiring which people live in brahmacarya.”',
 [('yat akṣaram veda-vidaḥ vadanti','<b>what knowers of the Veda call imperishable</b>'),('viśanti yat yatayaḥ vīta-rāgāḥ','<b>which ascetics free of passion enter</b>'),('yat icchantaḥ brahmacaryam caranti','<b>desiring which they live in brahmacarya</b>'),('tat te padam saṅgraheṇa pravakṣye','<b>that state I will tell you briefly</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa introduces the Imperishable goal, sought by sages.',
 'This verse closely echoes the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.15). Commentators note the three paths named: study, renunciation, celibate discipline.',
 'Different disciplines aim at the same imperishable goal.',
 'The goal <b>all sages seek</b>.'),
X(12,'CLOSING THE GATES',
 S('A yogi closing the gates of the body, mind gathered in the heart','Y','heart','CLOSE THE GATES'),
 '“Closing all the gates of the body, confining the mind in the heart, placing the life-breath in the head, established in yogic concentration,”',
 [('sarva-dvārāṇi saṃyamya','<b>controlling all the gates</b>'),('manaḥ hṛdi nirudhya ca','<b>confining the mind in the heart</b>'),('mūrdhni ādhāya ātmanaḥ prāṇam','<b>placing the life-breath in the head</b>'),('āsthitaḥ yoga-dhāraṇām','<b>established in yogic concentration</b>')],
 'The yogi’s method: close the senses, gather mind and breath.',
 'The “gates” are the senses (see 5.13’s city of nine gates). The sentence continues into 8.13.',
 'Closing the eyes and gathering the mind in the heart is a simple, powerful practice.',
 'Close the gates, <b>gather within</b>.'),
X(13,'OṂ AT THE END',
 S('A dying yogi chanting Om, rising to light','Y','om','OṂ'),
 '“…uttering Oṃ, the one-syllabled Brahman, and remembering me, whoever departs leaving the body reaches the supreme goal.”',
 [('om iti eka-akṣaram brahma','<b>Oṃ, the one-syllabled Brahman</b>'),('vyāharan mām anusmaran','<b>uttering, remembering me</b>'),('yaḥ prayāti tyajan deham','<b>who departs leaving the body</b>'),('saḥ yāti paramām gatim','<b>reaches the supreme goal</b>')],
 'Oṃ and remembrance at death lead to the highest.',
 'Oṃ is called Brahman in sound (Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad). Commentators say chanting it with remembrance unites word and meaning.',
 'Oṃ can be a daily companion, not only a final word.',
 '<b>Oṃ</b> and remembrance at the end.'),
X(14,'EASY FOR THE CONSTANT DEVOTEE',
 S('A devotee always thinking of Krishna, Krishna easily near','KA','heart','SULABHAḤ'),
 '“For the yogi who constantly remembers me, every day, with a mind that goes nowhere else, I am easy to reach, Pārtha.”',
 [('ananya-cetāḥ satatam','<b>with mind on nothing else, constantly</b>'),('yaḥ mām smarati nityaśaḥ','<b>who remembers me daily</b>'),('tasya aham su-labhaḥ','<b>for him I am easy to reach</b>'),('nitya-yuktasya yoginaḥ','<b>for the ever-united yogi</b>')],
 'After the hard yogic techniques, an easy promise: for the constant devotee, God is easy to reach.',
 '<i>Sulabha</i>, easily reached, is a key word in Rāmānuja’s tradition. Commentators contrast the difficult yogic death with the simple way of constant love.',
 'You don’t need complicated techniques. Constant loving remembrance is enough.',
 'For the devoted, <b>I am easy to reach</b>.'),
X(15,'NO MORE SORROW',
 S('Great souls reaching Krishna, leaving behind a world of sorrow','KA','light','MAHĀTMĀNAḤ'),
 '“Having reached me, the great souls, who have attained the highest perfection, do not return to birth, the impermanent home of sorrow.”',
 [('mām upetya punar-janma','<b>reaching me, rebirth</b>'),('duḥkha-ālayam aśāśvatam','<b>the impermanent home of sorrow</b>'),('na āpnuvanti mahā-ātmānaḥ','<b>the great souls do not get</b>'),('saṃsiddhim paramām gatāḥ','<b>having reached supreme perfection</b>')],
 'Reaching God ends rebirth.',
 '<i>Duḥkhālaya</i>, house of sorrow: the Gītā’s frank description of the world of rebirth. Commentators note the contrast: impermanent world, eternal goal.',
 'This world has much beauty, and also much sorrow. The Gītā points beyond both.',
 'Reaching me, <b>no return to sorrow</b>.'),
X(16,'EVEN BRAHMĀ’S WORLD RETURNS',
 S('Many heavenly worlds turning on a wheel, Krishna beyond them','','wheel','ALL RETURN'),
 '“All worlds, up to the world of Brahmā, return again, Arjuna. But on reaching me, son of Kuntī, there is no rebirth.”',
 [('ā-brahma-bhuvanāt lokāḥ','<b>worlds up to Brahmā’s</b>'),('punar-āvartinaḥ arjuna','<b>are subject to return</b>'),('mām upetya tu kaunteya','<b>but reaching me</b>'),('punar-janma na vidyate','<b>there is no rebirth</b>')],
 'Even the highest heavens are temporary.',
 'Commentators: heavens are rewards within the cycle; only reaching the Supreme ends the cycle. This puts all lesser goals in perspective.',
 'Even the best reward is temporary. Aim beyond rewards.',
 'Every heaven returns; <b>I do not</b>.'),
X(17,'A DAY OF BRAHMĀ',
 S('A giant clock with a thousand ages in a single day','','sun','1000 YUGAS'),
 '“Those who know that a day of Brahmā lasts a thousand ages, and his night a thousand ages, know day and night.”',
 [('sahasra-yuga-paryantam','<b>lasting a thousand yugas</b>'),('ahaḥ yat brahmaṇaḥ viduḥ','<b>who know the day of Brahmā</b>'),('rātrim yuga-sahasra-antām','<b>the night ending after a thousand yugas</b>'),('te ahaḥ-rātra-vidaḥ janāḥ','<b>they truly know day and night</b>')],
 'Cosmic time: Brahmā’s day and night last a thousand ages each.',
 'In Purāṇic reckoning a day of Brahmā (a kalpa) is about 4.32 billion human years. Commentators note the point: even the creator’s life has an end.',
 'Seen against cosmic time, our worries shrink. That can be freeing.',
 'A day of Brahmā: <b>a thousand ages</b>.'),
X(18,'DAWN AND NIGHT OF THE COSMOS',
 S('The universe appearing at Brahmā’s dawn','','star','DAWN'),
 '“At the coming of that day, all manifest things arise from the unmanifest; at the coming of night, they dissolve into that same unmanifest.”',
 [('avyaktāt vyaktayaḥ sarvāḥ','<b>from the unmanifest all manifest things</b>'),('prabhavanti ahar-āgame','<b>arise at the coming of day</b>'),('rātri-āgame pralīyante','<b>dissolve at the coming of night</b>'),('tatra eva avyakta-saṃjñake','<b>into that called the unmanifest</b>')],
 'The universe arises and dissolves in cycles.',
 'Commentators: creation is cyclic, not once and for all. The unmanifest is like a seed holding the tree.',
 'Endings are part of a cycle. Something always remains to begin again.',
 'Worlds rise and dissolve <b>in cycles</b>.'),
X(19,'AGAIN AND AGAIN',
 S('Beings appearing and disappearing in waves','','wheel','AGAIN AND AGAIN'),
 '“This same host of beings, coming into being again and again, dissolves helplessly at the coming of night, Pārtha, and arises at the coming of day.”',
 [('bhūta-grāmaḥ saḥ eva ayam','<b>this same multitude of beings</b>'),('bhūtvā bhūtvā pralīyate','<b>having arisen again and again, dissolves</b>'),('rātri-āgame avaśaḥ','<b>at night’s coming, helplessly</b>'),('prabhavati ahar-āgame','<b>arises at the day’s coming</b>')],
 'Beings are carried helplessly through the cycles.',
 '<i>Avaśa</i>, helpless: bound beings have no say in the cycle. Commentators contrast this with the freedom of reaching the Supreme.',
 'Much of life carries us along. Freedom means finding what is beyond the current.',
 'Carried <b>helplessly in the cycle</b>.'),
X(20,'BEYOND THE UNMANIFEST',
 S('A higher light beyond the cosmic seed, untouched by dissolution','','light','SANĀTANA'),
 '“But beyond that unmanifest there is another unmanifest, eternal, which does not perish when all beings perish.”',
 [('paraḥ tasmāt tu bhāvaḥ anyaḥ','<b>but beyond that, another state</b>'),('avyaktaḥ avyaktāt sanātanaḥ','<b>unmanifest, beyond the unmanifest, eternal</b>'),('yaḥ saḥ sarveṣu bhūteṣu','<b>which, when all beings</b>'),('naśyatsu na vinaśyati','<b>perish, does not perish</b>')],
 'Beyond the cosmic seed there is an eternal reality.',
 'Two unmanifests: the seed of the universe, which dissolves and returns; and the Supreme, which never perishes. Commentators say this is the goal.',
 'Behind all that comes and goes is what never goes.',
 'An eternal reality <b>beyond all cycles</b>.'),
X(21,'THE SUPREME ABODE',
 S('A shining abode at the summit, a door with no return','','om','PARAMAM DHĀMA'),
 '“That unmanifest is called the Imperishable; they call it the supreme goal. Reaching it, they do not return. That is my supreme abode.”',
 [('avyaktaḥ akṣaraḥ iti uktaḥ','<b>unmanifest, called the Imperishable</b>'),('tam āhuḥ paramām gatim','<b>they call it the supreme goal</b>'),('yam prāpya na nivartante','<b>reaching which they do not return</b>'),('tat dhāma paramam mama','<b>that is my supreme abode</b>')],
 'The goal named: the supreme abode.',
 '<i>Paramam dhāma</i>, the supreme abode, recurs in 15.6. Rāmānuja’s tradition understands it as Vaikuṇṭha; Śaṅkara as the state of Brahman.',
 'There is a home beyond all journeys.',
 'My supreme abode: <b>no return</b>.'),
X(22,'REACHED BY DEVOTION',
 S('A devotee whose love reaches Krishna who pervades all','KA','heart','ANANYA BHAKTI'),
 '“That supreme Person, Pārtha, in whom all beings dwell and by whom all this is pervaded, is reached by undivided devotion.”',
 [('puruṣaḥ saḥ paraḥ pārtha','<b>that supreme Person</b>'),('bhaktyā labhyaḥ tu ananyayā','<b>is reached by undivided devotion</b>'),('yasya antaḥ-sthāni bhūtāni','<b>in whom beings dwell</b>'),('yena sarvam idam tatam','<b>by whom all this is pervaded</b>')],
 'The way: undivided devotion.',
 '<i>Ananya bhakti</i>, devotion with no other object, is central to chapters 9–12. Commentators say this verse sums up the means after all the cosmology.',
 'Love with your whole heart. That is the way.',
 'Reached by <b>undivided devotion</b>.'),
X(23,'TWO PATHS AT DEATH',
 S('Two roads: one bright, one dark','K','sun','2 PATHS'),
 '“Now I will tell you, best of the Bharatas, the times in which yogis depart and do not return, and those in which they depart and return.”',
 [('yatra kāle tu anāvṛttim','<b>the time in which non-return</b>'),('āvṛttim ca eva yoginaḥ','<b>and return, yogis</b>'),('prayātāḥ yānti','<b>departing, go</b>'),('tam kālam vakṣyāmi','<b>that time I will tell</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa introduces the two paths of departure.',
 'This teaching comes from the Upaniṣads (Chāndogya 5.10, Bṛhadāraṇyaka 6.2). Commentators read the “times” as presiding deities of the path, not literal clock times.',
 'Old traditions describe journeys after death. The Gītā will soon say: the yogi need not worry.',
 'Two paths <b>after death</b>.'),
X(24,'THE BRIGHT PATH',
 S('Fire, light, day, bright fortnight and the northern sun leading upward','','fire','ŚUKLA'),
 '“Fire, light, day, the bright fortnight, the six months of the northern course: departing then, knowers of Brahman go to Brahman.”',
 [('agniḥ jyotiḥ ahaḥ śuklaḥ','<b>fire, light, day, the bright half</b>'),('ṣaṇmāsāḥ uttarāyaṇam','<b>six months of the northern course</b>'),('tatra prayātāḥ gacchanti','<b>departing there, they go</b>'),('brahma brahma-vidaḥ janāḥ','<b>knowers of Brahman to Brahman</b>')],
 'The path of light leads to Brahman.',
 'This is the <i>devayāna</i>, path of the gods. Bhīṣma waited for <i>uttarāyaṇa</i> before dying in the Mahābhārata. Commentators read these as stages guarded by deities of light.',
 'Live toward light, and the path will be bright.',
 'The bright path <b>leads to Brahman</b>.'),
X(25,'THE DARK PATH',
 S('Smoke, night, dark fortnight and the southern sun leading to the moon','','moon','KṚṢṆA'),
 '“Smoke, night, the dark fortnight, the six months of the southern course: departing then, the yogi reaches the light of the moon and returns.”',
 [('dhūmaḥ rātriḥ tathā kṛṣṇaḥ','<b>smoke, night, the dark half</b>'),('ṣaṇmāsāḥ dakṣiṇāyanam','<b>six months of the southern course</b>'),('tatra cāndramasam jyotiḥ','<b>there the light of the moon</b>'),('yogī prāpya nivartate','<b>the yogi reaching, returns</b>')],
 'The path of smoke leads to the lunar heaven and back to birth.',
 'This is the <i>pitṛyāna</i>, path of the ancestors, for those who did good works with desire. Commentators: a good path, but it returns.',
 'Good works done for reward bring rewards, and then return.',
 'The dark path <b>returns</b>.'),
X(26,'ETERNAL PATHS',
 S('Two paths, one going on, one curving back','','wheel','ŚUKLA · KṚṢṆA'),
 '“These two paths of the world, the bright and the dark, are considered eternal. By one, one goes not to return; by the other, one returns again.”',
 [('śukla-kṛṣṇe gatī hi ete','<b>these two paths, bright and dark</b>'),('jagataḥ śāśvate mate','<b>of the world, considered eternal</b>'),('ekayā yāti anāvṛttim','<b>by one, one goes to non-return</b>'),('anyayā āvartate punaḥ','<b>by the other, returns again</b>')],
 'Two eternal paths: one to freedom, one back to birth.',
 'Commentators simplify: the bright path of knowledge and devotion, and the dark path of desire-bound works.',
 'Every choice leans toward one path or the other.',
 'Two paths: <b>freedom or return</b>.'),
X(27,'THE YOGI IS NOT CONFUSED',
 S('A calm yogi unconcerned about the path, knowing the way','Y','light','NA MUHYATI'),
 '“Knowing these two paths, Pārtha, no yogi is deluded. Therefore at all times be steadfast in yoga, Arjuna.”',
 [('na ete sṛtī pārtha jānan','<b>knowing these two paths</b>'),('yogī muhyati kaścana','<b>no yogi is deluded</b>'),('tasmāt sarveṣu kāleṣu','<b>therefore at all times</b>'),('yoga-yuktaḥ bhava arjuna','<b>be steadfast in yoga</b>')],
 'The practical conclusion: don’t worry about times; be steady in yoga always.',
 'Commentators say the yogi need not wait for an auspicious moment; constant yoga makes every moment right.',
 'Don’t wait for the perfect time. Live well now.',
 'Be steady <b>at all times</b>.'),
X(28,'BEYOND ALL MERITS',
 S('A yogi passing beyond Vedas, sacrifices, austerities and gifts to the supreme abode','Y','om','BEYOND ALL'),
 '“Whatever fruit of merit is declared for the Vedas, sacrifices, austerities and gifts, the yogi who knows this goes beyond it all and reaches the supreme, primal abode.”',
 [('vedeṣu yajñeṣu tapaḥsu','<b>in Vedas, sacrifices, austerities</b>'),('dāneṣu yat puṇya-phalam','<b>in gifts, whatever fruit of merit</b>'),('atyeti tat sarvam idam viditvā','<b>goes beyond all that, knowing this</b>'),('yogī param sthānam upaiti ādyam','<b>the yogi reaches the supreme, primal abode</b>')],
 'The yogi transcends all merits.',
 'Commentators: the merit of good works is good but limited; the yogi aims beyond merit, at the source itself.',
 'Good works are good. Love and knowledge of the source are better still.',
 'The yogi goes <b>beyond all merit</b>.'),
]
